<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Clandeboye</h1><p class="st-meta">1350 CE – 1605 CE (approx.) · capital Castlereagh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Gaelic lordship in Antrim and Down held by the Clann Aodha Buidhe O'Neills, who took over the lands of the collapsed Earldom of Ulster.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clandeboye-oneills">Clann Aodha Buidhe</a>, descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aodh-buidhe-oneill">Aodh Buidhe O'Neill</a>, a 13th century king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tir-eoghain">Tir Eoghain</a>, moved east across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-bann">Bann</a> after the collapse of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/earldom-of-ulster">Earldom of Ulster</a> in 1333. By around 1350 they ruled much of south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-antrim">Antrim</a> and north Down, later divided into Upper and Lower Clandeboye.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lordship's seat was the castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castlereagh">Castlereagh</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a>, and its chiefs ruled through Gaelic law, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> wealth and Scottish gallowglasses. Their territory was later contested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macdonnells-of-antrim">MacDonnells</a> and by English colonisation attempts, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-devereux-1st-earl-of-essex">Earl of Essex</a>'s massacre of Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-macphelim-oneill">Brian MacPhelim O'Neill</a> and his family in 1574.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/con-oneill-of-clandeboye">Con O'Neill</a>, the last lord, lost most of his lands to the Scots <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-montgomery-1st-viscount-montgomery">Hugh Montgomery</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-hamilton-1st-viscount-claneboye">James Hamilton</a> in 1605, opening north Down to large-scale Scottish settlement.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lordship-of-clandeboye.jpg" alt="Lordship of Clandeboye" width="320" height="267"><figcaption>Image: Mabuska (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulster_Early_16th_Century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1350 CE – 1605 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Castlereagh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Brian MacPhelim O'Neill, Con O'Neill</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Irish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United Kingdom</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Clann Aodha Buidhe, Upper and Lower Clandeboye</dd></dl></aside></div>
